---
source: crates/forge_domain/src/tools/definition/usage.rs
expression: prompt
---
<tool>{"name":"read","description":"Read one file: text, PDF, image, or notebook. Relative paths resolve against the working directory.\n- Text comes back as `12:content`: line number, a colon, then the text. Lines are returned whole, however long they are. The prefix is not file content; strip it when you quote text back into an edit. `show_line_numbers: false` returns the text without prefixes.\n- A read stops after {{config.maxReadSize}} lines and ends with a footer naming the exact `range` that continues it. Use `range` with `start_line`/`end_line` for a slice of the file.\n- A whole-file read (no `range`) of a file larger than {{config.maxReadBytes}} bytes is refused with a suggested ranged call; ranged reads are not subject to that cap.\n- `range` also takes a byte range, `start_byte` (0-based) and optional `end_byte` (one past the last byte), for text with few or no line breaks: it returns raw text without line prefixes, one page at a time, snapped to UTF-8 character boundaries, and names the offset to continue from. Use it to page through a saved result file.\n- Pass either a line range or a byte range, never both.\n- Reading the same lines of an unchanged file again, while the earlier result is still in the conversation, returns a short note naming the read call that showed them.\n- A Jupyter notebook (.ipynb) read without a range comes back as its cells, each with its id, type, source and a summary of its outputs; a line or byte range reads its raw JSON.\n- A PDF comes back as extracted text (via `pdftotext`).\n{{#if (contains model.input_modalities \"image\")}}\n- Images (PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP) come back as images; a PDF with no extractable text comes back as an image. Images and image-fallback PDFs larger than {{config.maxImageSize}} bytes are refused.\n{{/if}}\n- Directories, pipes and other special files are refused{{#if tool_names.fs_search}}; use `{{tool_names.fs_search}}` to find files{{/if}}.\n- Reading records which lines you have seen.{{#if tool_names.write}} An overwriting `{{tool_names.write}}` requires a prior read of the file.{{/if}}{{#if tool_names.patch}} `{{tool_names.patch}}` requires a prior read and that every line it changes was shown.{{/if}}\n- You can call multiple tools in a single response; read several files in parallel when you need them.","arguments":{"file_path":{"description":"Path of the file to read.","type":"string","is_required":true},"range":{"description":"Optional line range (`start_line`/`end_line`) or byte range\n(`start_byte`/`end_byte`) for partial reads.","type":"object","is_required":false},"show_line_numbers":{"description":"If true, prefixes each line with its line number. Defaults to true.","type":"boolean","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"write","description":"Create a file, or replace one whole. Missing parent directories are created.\n- Replacing an existing file needs `overwrite=true` and a prior read of the file in this conversation{{#if tool_names.read}} (`{{tool_names.read}}`){{/if}}; one page is enough, the whole file is not needed. Without `overwrite=true` an existing file is refused.\n- If the file changed on disk since that read, the overwrite is refused and nothing is written; the error names both hashes and shows the diff from what you read to what is there now, and a retry after it goes through.\n- The file keeps its existing line-ending style (LF, CRLF or CR); a new file gets LF.\n- The previous state is snapshotted first{{#if tool_names.undo}}, so `{{tool_names.undo}}` brings it back (undoing a create deletes the file){{/if}}.\n- After the write the file is parsed locally and syntax errors the write introduced are reported as a warning.\n{{#if tool_names.patch}}\n- Edit existing files with `{{tool_names.patch}}`; use this only for new files or a deliberate full rewrite.\n{{/if}}\n- Do not create documentation files unless asked.","arguments":{"content":{"description":"The complete file content to write.","type":"string","is_required":true},"file_path":{"description":"Path of the file to create or replace.","type":"string","is_required":true},"overwrite":{"description":"Required to replace an existing file; the file must have been read\nfirst and must not have changed since.","type":"boolean","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"fs_search","description":"Search file contents by regex. `output_mode` is `files_with_matches` (paths, the default), `content` (matching lines as `path:line:text`, context lines as `path-line-text`, `--` between groups) or `count` (`path:N`).\n- The search uses ripgrep's libraries in-process. Paths ignored by `.gitignore`/`.ignore` are skipped, as are hidden paths unless `path` names one or `glob` names a dot-leading component such as `.github/**`; binary files are skipped.\n- `glob` filters paths (a leading `!` excludes); `file_type` takes ripgrep type names (`rust`, `py`, `js`, …) or a bare extension, and cannot be combined with a positive `glob`.\n- Regex syntax is ripgrep's: escape literal braces (`interface\\{\\}`); `multiline: true` lets a pattern span lines.\n- Results are absolute paths in path order. Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 are shown as `\\xNN`.\n- The walk stays on the filesystem `path` is on and does not enter mount points below it; name a mounted share as `path` to search it. A search is bounded at 120 seconds.\n- `head_limit` and `offset` page through a large result; the result names the next `offset` when there are more. A result too large to show inline is written to a file whose path the result names.\n{{#if tool_names.read}}\n- Use `{{tool_names.read}}` to see a file once a search has found it.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if tool_names.task}}\n- Use `{{tool_names.task}}` for open-ended investigations that need several rounds of search and reading.\n{{/if}}","arguments":{"case_insensitive":{"description":"Match without case sensitivity.","type":"boolean","is_required":false},"context":{"description":"Lines of context before and after each match (content mode).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"context_after":{"description":"Lines of context after each match (content mode).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"context_before":{"description":"Lines of context before each match (content mode).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"file_type":{"description":"File type, e.g. rust, py, js (ripgrep type names).","type":"string","is_required":false},"glob":{"description":"Glob filter on paths (e.g. \"*.rs\", \"*.{ts,tsx}\", \"!*.min.js\").","type":"string","is_required":false},"head_limit":{"description":"Maximum result entries after `offset`.","type":"integer","is_required":false},"line_numbers":{"description":"Prefix content lines with their line numbers (default true).","type":"boolean","is_required":false},"multiline":{"description":"Let the regex span lines (`.` matches newlines).","type":"boolean","is_required":false},"offset":{"description":"Result entries to skip before `head_limit`.","type":"integer","is_required":false},"output_mode":{"description":"\"content\" (matching lines), \"files_with_matches\" (paths, default) or\n\"count\" (matches per file).","type":"string","is_required":false},"path":{"description":"File or directory to search (default: the current directory).","type":"string","is_required":false},"pattern":{"description":"Regex to search file contents for.","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"ast_search","description":"Search code structurally with ast-grep (in-process, tree-sitter grammars). Patterns match parsed syntax, so they span whitespace and line breaks and never match inside comments or strings.{{#if tool_names.fs_search}} Use `{{tool_names.fs_search}}` for plain text, regex and filenames.{{/if}}\n- In a pattern `$NAME` matches one syntax node and `$$$NAME` matches zero or more; metavariables are uppercase, and reusing one requires equal captures.\n- The language is inferred from each file's extension; set `language` to restrict the search to one language. Under `language=\"c\"` a bare call pattern like `call($A)` matches nothing, because tree-sitter-c parses that fragment as a type; write it as an expression statement (`call($A);`).\n- Matches come back as an absolute `path:line` heading, the numbered source lines (`12|code`), and the metavariable captures. When more matches exist than `max_results` (default 200), the extra ones are dropped and the result says so.\n- The walk skips paths ignored by `.gitignore` and hidden paths.\n- Rewrites are two-step: `rewrite` alone is a dry run that shows a unified diff and changes nothing; `apply=true` writes it and is refused when there are more matches than `max_results`. An applied rewrite snapshots every changed file first{{#if tool_names.undo}}, so `{{tool_names.undo}}` brings each back{{/if}}.","arguments":{"apply":{"description":"Write the rewrite (default false).","type":"boolean","is_required":false},"language":{"description":"Language (e.g. rust, python, typescript, tsx, go, c, cpp); inferred\nfrom each file's extension when omitted.","type":"string","is_required":false},"max_results":{"description":"Most matches reported (default 200); an apply with more matches is\nrefused.","type":"integer","is_required":false},"path":{"description":"File or directory to search (default: the current directory).","type":"string","is_required":false},"pattern":{"description":"ast-grep pattern; `$NAME` matches one node, `$$$NAME` zero or more.","type":"string","is_required":true},"rewrite":{"description":"Replacement template using the pattern's metavariables; without\n`apply` the result is a diff and nothing is written.","type":"string","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"remove","description":"Move a file, directory or symlink to the trash, or delete it with `permanent=true`. Either way the path is snapshotted first{{#if tool_names.undo}}, so `{{tool_names.undo}}` can bring it back{{/if}}. Symlinks are removed as symlinks, never followed. The trash uses `trash` or `trash-put`; when neither is installed or the command fails, nothing is removed and `permanent=true` is the way to remove the path. Anything over 512 MiB is refused rather than trashed; confirm with the user before deleting that with `permanent=true`.","arguments":{"path":{"description":"Path of the file, directory or symlink to remove.","type":"string","is_required":true},"permanent":{"description":"Delete directly instead of moving to the trash (default false).","type":"boolean","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"patch","description":"Replace exact text in one file.\n- Read the file{{#if tool_names.read}} with `{{tool_names.read}}`{{/if}} before editing it; editing a file this conversation has not read is refused. The `12:` prefix in read output is not file content; only the text after it is.\n- `old_string` must match exactly once unless `replace_all` is set. When it is not in the file exactly, it is matched again with smart quotes, Unicode dashes, no-break spaces and trailing whitespace read as plain ASCII; the file keeps its own characters wherever your edit left `old_string` unchanged. With `replace_all`, this fallback runs only when there is no exact occurrence. A miss reports the closest line in the file.\n- Pass `edits` (a list of `{old_string, new_string, replace_all}`) for several replacements in one call: they apply in order, each seeing the previous result, and all are validated before anything is written, so a failed call leaves the file untouched and names the offending edit. Never combine `edits` with the scalar `old_string`/`new_string`/`replace_all` form.\n- Line endings in `old_string`/`new_string` are matched and written in the file's own style (LF or CRLF).\n- After the write, the file is parsed locally and any syntax errors the edit introduced are reported as a warning.\n{{#if tool_names.undo}}\n- One call is one `{{tool_names.undo}}` step.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if tool_names.write}}\n- Use `{{tool_names.write}}` for new files or a deliberate full rewrite.\n{{/if}}","arguments":{"edits":{"description":"Several replacements applied in order, each seeing the previous\nresult. Never combined with the scalar form.","type":"array","is_required":false},"file_path":{"description":"Path of the file to modify.","type":"string","is_required":true},"new_string":{"description":"Replacement text; must differ from old_string (scalar form).","type":"string","is_required":false},"old_string":{"description":"Exact text to replace (scalar form).","type":"string","is_required":false},"replace_all":{"description":"Replace every occurrence of old_string instead of requiring exactly\none (scalar form, default false).","type":"boolean","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"undo","description":"Restore one path to its latest snapshot. Every tool that changes a file snapshots it first{{#if tool_names.patch}}; one `{{tool_names.patch}}` call, even with several edits, is one snapshot{{/if}}.\n- Restores file contents and permission modes, symlink targets, whole directory trees, and absence: undoing the creation of a file deletes it. Ownership, timestamps and extended attributes are not restored.\n- Each successful restore pops one snapshot, so repeated calls walk further back. A filesystem error keeps the snapshot for a retry. An unusable snapshot is discarded with an error without changing the path; call again to reach an older entry.\n- Snapshots belong to this conversation and survive a restart of it; the store keeps the newest 100 entries (64 MiB). This is not git and does not touch repository history.","arguments":{"path":{"description":"Path whose latest snapshot should be restored.","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"shell","description":"Run one command with `{{env.shell}}` and return its exit code, stdout and stderr. Under bash and zsh a pipeline fails when any stage fails (`pipefail`), so `false | cat` reports exit 1. The working directory is `cwd`, default `{{env.cwd}}`; set `cwd` instead of using `cd`.\n\nLong output comes back as its head and its tail, with a marker between them naming the bytes left out and the file that holds the whole stream:\n\n    [truncated: output budget (… bytes) reached; stdout bytes 25000-980000 of 1004000 (955000 bytes) are not shown; the whole stdout is at /path/shell-3-….log — read it on with range {\"start_byte\": 25000}]\n\nRead any part of that file{{#if tool_names.read}} with `{{tool_names.read}}` and a byte range{{/if}}, so do not pipe through `head` or `tail` just to shrink the output. Stdin is `/dev/null` and there is no terminal: a command that reads input gets end-of-file, and a password or confirmation prompt gets no answer.\n\nA command does not have to finish before you get an answer. The call waits `timeout` seconds (default 30); a command that finishes inside that window comes back whole, and one still running comes back as a handle with whatever it has printed so far:\n\n    command still running after 30s (handle 3, pid 4242). ...\n    HANDLE 3 RUNNING\n\nThe command is never killed by the wait. Then `action=\"poll\", handle=\"3\"` returns new output and whether it is still running, `action=\"wait\", handle=\"3\", timeout=N` blocks up to N more seconds, and `action=\"kill\", handle=\"3\"` stops it (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL, to its whole process group). `handle` defaults to the most recent running job. Do a poll before assuming a long build or test run has failed. Interrupting the turn kills a command the call is still waiting on.\n\nThe command gets a filtered environment: the configured allow-list (by default PATH, HOME, USER, LANG, LC_ALL, TERM, PWD, SHELL) plus the names you list in `env`. When the command references a variable that was filtered out, the result says so.\n\n- Independent commands can go in parallel calls in one response; chain dependent ones with `&&` in one call.\n{{#if tool_names.fs_search}}\n- Search with `{{tool_names.fs_search}}`, not `grep`, `rg`, or `find`.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if tool_names.read}}\n- Read files with `{{tool_names.read}}`, not `cat`, `head`, or `tail`.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if tool_names.patch}}\n- Edit files with `{{tool_names.patch}}`, not `sed` or `awk`.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if tool_names.write}}\n- Write files with `{{tool_names.write}}`, not redirects or heredocs.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if tool_names.remove}}\n- Delete with `{{tool_names.remove}}`, not `rm`.\n{{/if}}","arguments":{"action":{"description":"run (default), poll, wait or kill.","is_required":false},"command":{"description":"The command to run (required for action \"run\").","type":"string","is_required":false},"cwd":{"description":"Working directory for the command (default: the current directory).","type":"string","is_required":false},"description":{"description":"Short description of what the command does.","type":"string","is_required":false},"env":{"description":"Extra environment variable names to pass through, on top of the\nconfigured allow-list.","type":"array","is_required":false},"handle":{"description":"Job id from a `HANDLE n RUNNING` line, for poll/wait/kill (default:\nthe most recent running job).","type":"string","is_required":false},"keep_ansi":{"description":"Keep ANSI escape codes in the output (default false).","type":"boolean","is_required":false},"timeout":{"description":"Seconds to wait before returning a handle (run) or to keep waiting\n(wait); default 30. The command is never killed when the window ends.","type":"integer","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"fetch","description":"Fetch a URL over HTTP(S) or `file://`.\n\nHTML comes back as markdown with links made absolute, so you can fetch them as-is; `raw=true` returns the source instead. `method`, `headers`, `body` and `json_body` make any HTTP request (a GET that saves nothing checks robots.txt first). `save` streams the body to that path instead of returning it, with no size limit; a partial download left by an interrupted save resumes with a Range request, and the previous file at that path is snapshotted first{{#if tool_names.undo}} (`{{tool_names.undo}}` brings it back){{/if}}. Binary responses come back as a short descriptor naming the type and size; pass `save` to get the bytes. Images come back as images when the model accepts image input. Unsaved responses are read up to 32 MiB and bounded by a 30-second timeout; a large text result is written to a file whose path and continuation range the result names.","arguments":{"body":{"description":"Request body text.","type":"string","is_required":false},"headers":{"description":"Request headers, name to value.","type":"object","is_required":false},"json_body":{"description":"Request body as JSON (sets Content-Type: application/json); not with\n`body`.","type":"object","is_required":false},"method":{"description":"HTTP method (default GET).","type":"string","is_required":false},"raw":{"description":"Return the body as is, without HTML-to-markdown conversion (default\nfalse).","type":"boolean","is_required":false},"save":{"description":"Stream the response body to this path instead of returning it; no size\nlimit, resumable.","type":"string","is_required":false},"url":{"description":"URL to fetch: http(s):// or file://.","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"plan","description":"Write a markdown plan to `plans/{date}-{plan_name}-{version}.md` under the working directory (or the configured plans directory).\n- Use it for durable implementation plans, task breakdowns, investigation notes or strategy documents that should outlive this turn, or when the user asks for a plan to be written down. Do not create documentation proactively.\n- `plan_name` and `version` are sanitised into the filename: anything other than letters, digits, `-`, `_` and `.` becomes `-`.\n- `content` is written verbatim.\n- An existing plan with the same date, name and version is left unchanged unless `overwrite=true`; an overwrite reports that it replaced the plan{{#if tool_names.undo}}, and the previous contents are snapshotted so `{{tool_names.undo}}` brings them back{{/if}}.\n- This is for plans, not source-code edits.","arguments":{"content":{"description":"The complete plan content in markdown, written verbatim.","type":"string","is_required":true},"overwrite":{"description":"Replace an existing plan with the same name and version (default\nfalse).","type":"boolean","is_required":false},"plan_name":{"description":"The name of the plan (sanitised into the filename).","type":"string","is_required":true},"version":{"description":"The version of the plan (e.g., \"v1\", \"v2\", \"1.0\"; sanitised into the\nfilename).","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"skill","description":"Fetches detailed information about a specific skill. Use this tool to load skill content and instructions when you need to understand how to perform a specialized task. Skills provide domain-specific knowledge, workflows, and best practices. Only invoke skills that are listed in the available skills section. Do not invoke a skill that is already active.","arguments":{"name":{"description":"The name of the skill to fetch (e.g., \"pdf\", \"code_review\")","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"todo_write","description":"Use this tool to create and manage a structured task list for your current coding session. This helps you track progress, organize complex tasks, and demonstrate thoroughness to the user.\nIt also helps the user understand the progress of the task and overall progress of their requests.\n\n## How It Works\n\nEach call sends only the items that changed — you do not need to repeat the whole list.\n\nEach item has two required fields:\n- `content`: The task description. This is the **unique key** — the server matches on content to decide whether to add or update.\n- `status`: One of `pending`, `in_progress`, `completed`, or `cancelled`.\n\n**Rules:**\n- Item with this `content` does **not** exist yet → **added** as a new task.\n- Item with this `content` already exists → its `status` is **updated**.\n- `status: cancelled` → the item is **removed** from the list entirely.\n- Items you do not mention are **left unchanged**.\n\nIDs are managed internally by the system and are never exposed to you.\n\n## When to Use This Tool\nUse this tool proactively in these scenarios:\n\n1. Complex multi-step tasks - When a task requires 3 or more distinct steps or actions\n2. Non-trivial and complex tasks - Tasks that require careful planning or multiple operations\n3. User explicitly requests todo list - When the user directly asks you to use the todo list\n4. User provides multiple tasks - When users provide a list of things to be done (numbered or comma-separated)\n5. After receiving new instructions - Immediately capture user requirements as todos\n6. When you start working on a task - Mark it as in_progress BEFORE beginning work. Ideally you should only have one todo as in_progress at a time\n7. After completing a task - Mark it as completed and add any new follow-up tasks discovered during implementation\n\n## When NOT to Use This Tool\n\nSkip using this tool when:\n1. There is only a single, straightforward task\n2. The task is trivial and tracking it provides no organizational benefit\n3. The task can be completed in less than 3 trivial steps\n4. The task is purely conversational or informational\n\nNOTE that you should not use this tool if there is only one trivial task to do. In this case you are better off just doing the task directly.\n\n## Examples of When to Use the Todo List\n\n<example>\nUser: I want to add a dark mode toggle to the application settings. Make sure you run the tests and build when you're done!\nAssistant: I'll help add a dark mode toggle to your application settings. Let me create a todo list to track this implementation.\n*Creates todo list with the following items:*\n1. Creating dark mode toggle component in Settings page\n2. Adding dark mode state management (context/store)\n3. Implementing CSS-in-JS styles for dark theme\n4. Updating existing components to support theme switching\n5. Running tests and build process, addressing any failures or errors that occur\n*Begins working on the first task*\n\n<reasoning>\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\n1. Adding dark mode is a multi-step feature requiring UI, state management, and styling changes\n2. The user explicitly requested tests and build be run afterward\n3. The assistant inferred that tests and build need to pass by adding \"Ensure tests and build succeed\" as the final task\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\nUser: Help me rename the function getCwd to getCurrentWorkingDirectory across my project\nAssistant: Let me first search through your codebase to find all occurrences of 'getCwd'.\n*Uses grep or search tools to locate all instances of getCwd in the codebase*\nAssistant: I've found 15 instances of 'getCwd' across 8 different files. Let me create a todo list to track these changes.\n*Creates todo list with specific items for each file that needs updating*\n\n<reasoning>\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\n1. First, the assistant searched to understand the scope of the task\n2. Upon finding multiple occurrences across different files, it determined this was a complex task with multiple steps\n3. The todo list helps ensure every instance is tracked and updated systematically\n4. This approach prevents missing any occurrences and maintains code consistency\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n\n<example>\nUser: I need to implement these features for my e-commerce site: user registration, product catalog, shopping cart, and checkout flow.\nAssistant: I'll help implement these features. First, let's add all the features to the todo list.\n*Creates a todo list breaking down each feature into specific tasks based on the project architecture*\nAssistant: Let's start with user registration. This will involve creating database models, API endpoints, and frontend forms that integrate with your existing authentication system.\n\n<reasoning>\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\n1. The user provided multiple complex features to implement in a comma separated list\n2. The todo list helps organize these large features into manageable tasks\n3. This approach allows for tracking progress across the entire implementation\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\nUser: Can you help optimize my React application? It's rendering slowly and has performance issues.</user>\nAssistant: I'll help optimize your React application. First, let me examine your codebase to identify potential performance bottlenecks.\n*Reviews component structure, render patterns, state management, and data fetching*\nAssistant: After analyzing your codebase, I've identified several performance issues. Let me create a todo list to track our optimization efforts.\n*Creates todo list with items like: 1) Implementing memoization for expensive calculations in ProductList, 2) Adding virtualization for long lists in Dashboard, 3) Optimizing image loading in Gallery component, 4) Fixing state update loops in ShoppingCart, 5) Reviewing bundle size and implementing code splitting*\nLet's start by implementing memoization for the expensive calculations in your ProductList component.</assistant>\n\n<reasoning>\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\n1. First, the assistant examined the codebase to identify specific performance issues\n2. Based on this analysis, it identified multiple optimization opportunities\n3. Performance optimization is a non-trivial task requiring multiple steps\n4. The todo list helps methodically track improvements across different components\n5. This systematic approach ensures all performance bottlenecks are addressed\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n## Examples of When NOT to Use the Todo List\n\n<example>\nUser: How do I print 'Hello World' in Python?\nAssistant: In Python, you can print \"Hello World\" with this simple code:\n\npython\nprint(\"Hello World\")\n\nThis will output the text \"Hello World\" to the console when executed.</assistant>\n\n<reasoning>\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is a single, trivial task that can be completed in one step. There's no need to track multiple tasks or steps for such a straightforward request.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\nUser: What does the git status command do?\nAssistant: The git status command shows the current state of your working directory and staging area. It displays which changes have been staged, which haven't, and which files aren't being tracked by Git. This is a fundamental command for understanding the current state of your repository before making commits.\n\n<reasoning>\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is an informational request with no actual coding task to complete. The user is simply asking for an explanation, not for the assistant to perform multiple steps or tasks.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\nUser: Can you add a comment to the calculateTotal function to explain what it does?\nAssistant: Sure, let me add a comment to the calculateTotal function to explain what it does.\n* Uses the patch tool to add a comment to the calculateTotal function *\n\n<reasoning>\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is a single, straightforward task confined to one location in the code. Adding a comment doesn't require tracking multiple steps or systematic organization.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\nUser: Run npm install for me and tell me what happens.\nAssistant: I'll run the npm install command for you.\n\n*Executes: npm install*\n\nThe command completed successfully. Here's the output:\n[Output of npm install command]\n\nAll dependencies have been installed according to your package.json file.\n\n<reasoning>\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is a single command execution with immediate results. There are no multiple steps to track or organize, making the todo list unnecessary for this straightforward task.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n## Task States and Management\n\n1. **Task States**: Use these states to track progress:\n   - `pending`: Task not yet started\n   - `in_progress`: Currently working on (limit to ONE task at a time)\n   - `completed`: Task finished successfully\n   - `cancelled`: Task is no longer relevant — this removes it from the list\n\n2. **Task Management**:\n   - Only send the items that changed — do not repeat unchanged items\n   - Mark tasks `in_progress` BEFORE beginning work\n   - Mark tasks `completed` IMMEDIATELY after finishing (don't batch completions)\n   - Exactly ONE task must be `in_progress` at any time\n   - Use `cancelled` to remove tasks that are no longer relevant\n   - Complete current tasks before starting new ones\n\n3. **Task Completion Requirements**:\n   - ONLY mark a task as `completed` when you have FULLY accomplished it\n   - If you encounter errors, blockers, or cannot finish, keep the task as `in_progress`\n   - When blocked, create a new task describing what needs to be resolved\n   - Never mark a task as `completed` if:\n     - Tests are failing\n     - Implementation is partial\n     - You encountered unresolved errors\n     - You couldn't find necessary files or dependencies\n\n4. **Task Breakdown**:\n   - Create specific, actionable items\n   - Break complex tasks into smaller, manageable steps\n   - Use clear, descriptive task names\n\nWhen in doubt, use this tool. Being proactive with task management demonstrates attentiveness and ensures you complete all requirements successfully.","arguments":{"todos":{"description":"List of todo items to create or update. Each item must have `content`\nand `status`. The server matches on `content` — if an item with the\nsame content exists it is updated; otherwise a new item is added.\nSet `status` to `cancelled` to remove an item.","type":"array","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"todo_read","description":"Retrieves the current todo list for this coding session. Use this tool to check existing todos before making updates, or to review the current state of tasks at any point during the session.\n\n## When to Use This Tool\n\n- Before calling `todo_write`, to understand which tasks already exist and avoid duplicates\n- When you need to know what tasks are pending, in progress, or completed\n- To resume work after a break and understand the current state of tasks\n- When the user asks about the current task list or progress\n\n## Output\n\nReturns all current todos with their IDs, content, and status (`pending`, `in_progress`, `completed`). If no todos exist yet, returns an empty list.","arguments":{}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"task","description":"Launch one or more worker agents for complex, multi-step work. Each worker runs autonomously in its own conversation and returns one result to you; the result is not shown to the user, so summarize it in your reply.\n\nAvailable agents and their tools:\n{{#each agents}}\n- **{{id}}**{{#if description}}: {{description}}{{/if}}{{#if tools}}\n  - Tools: {{#each tools}}{{this}}{{#unless @last}}, {{/unless}}{{/each}}{{/if}}\n{{/each}}\n\nWhen to use: open-ended searches that need several search/read rounds, independent investigations that can run concurrently, work that suits another agent's persona and tools.\nWhen not to use: anything one or two direct tool calls finish{{#if tool_names.read}}, such as reading one known file or two or three files (use `{{tool_names.read}}`){{/if}}{{#if tool_names.fs_search}}, or searching for an exact identifier or definition (use `{{tool_names.fs_search}}`){{/if}}.\n\nInputs:\n- `tasks` (required to start workers): one self-contained prompt per worker. A fresh worker cannot see this conversation; include paths, error text, constraints, the expected output shape, and whether it may change code.\n- `agent_id` (required to start workers): the agent the workers run as. An unknown agent is an error and no worker starts.\n- `session_id`: resume a worker session (the id is named in an earlier result as `session_id=…`). It runs exactly one task; an unknown id is an error.\n- `model`: run the workers on another model. Leave it unset unless the user asks for it.\n\nParallelism: the tasks of one call run concurrently through a bounded worker pool, and several `{{tool_names.task}}` calls in one response also run concurrently. One task returns the worker's reply verbatim; several come back numbered under a `TASK_RESULTS` header in task order. A worker that fails reports its error in its own slot without discarding the others. Each reply is held to an equal share of the result budget. Workers that start their own tasks run one level deeper; past the configured depth limit a call is refused.\n\nBackground: a call blocks until every worker finishes. With `background=true` it returns at once with a handle while the workers keep running:\n\n    task running in the background (handle t1): ...\n    HANDLE t1 RUNNING\n\n`action=\"poll\", handle=\"t1\"` says whether it is still running and returns the result once done; `action=\"wait\", handle=\"t1\", timeout=N` blocks up to N seconds, or until the end without `timeout`; `action=\"kill\", handle=\"t1\"` stops it. `handle` defaults to your most recent running task, and you see only tasks this conversation started. The finished result is also written to the file the handle names, and if you have not polled it by your next turn, a reminder names that file. Use background runs for slow, independent work you do not need before your next step.","arguments":{"action":{"description":"run (default), poll, wait or kill; poll/wait/kill act on a background\ntask's handle.","is_required":false},"agent_id":{"description":"The agent the workers run as (required to start workers).","type":"string","is_required":false},"background":{"description":"Run the workers in the background and return a handle at once.","type":"boolean","is_required":false},"handle":{"description":"Handle of a background task (default: your most recent running one).","type":"string","is_required":false},"model":{"description":"Model the workers run on instead of the agent's configured model. Leave\nunset unless the user asks for it.","type":"string","is_required":false},"session_id":{"description":"Resume an earlier worker session (one task only). An unknown id is an\nerror.","type":"string","is_required":false},"tasks":{"description":"Self-contained prompts, one per worker; they run in parallel (required\nto start workers).","type":"array","is_required":false},"timeout":{"description":"Seconds a wait blocks (default: until the task ends).","type":"integer","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"tool_discovery","description":"Search the catalog of tools and skills this agent may load but does not have yet, or load one by its id.\n- An empty `query` returns a compact index (ids, names, sources; capped at 4 KiB). A search ranks rows by BM25 over names, descriptions and parameter names and adds short descriptions (capped at 8 KiB). `kind` (native, mcp, skill) and an exact `source` narrow the rows. When `truncated` is true, repeat the call with `offset` set to `next_offset`.\n- `load` takes the exact `id` of a row whose `loadable` is true. Loading a tool publishes its full schema from the next model call on, for the rest of this conversation. Loading a skill returns its instructions and loads the tools it declares that this agent may use. Searching with `kind=\"mcp\"` or an MCP server's name as `source` connects to those servers first; a server that is not connected yet or failed to connect appears as a status row, which is not loadable.\n- Load first, then call the tool in a later response: a tool is not callable in the same response that loads it. Calling a tool that is not loaded returns an error naming the exact load call.","arguments":{"kind":{"description":"Only rows of this kind: native, mcp or skill.","type":"string","is_required":false},"load":{"description":"Catalog id to load, such as `native:lsp`, `mcp:github__list_issues`\nor `skill:review`.","type":"string","is_required":false},"offset":{"description":"Continue a truncated result at its `next_offset`.","type":"integer","is_required":false},"query":{"description":"Intent or words to search for; empty returns a compact index.","type":"string","is_required":false},"source":{"description":"Only rows with exactly this source (an MCP server name, `native`, or\na skill source).","type":"string","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"mcp_resource_list","description":"List the resources of an MCP server. Before using it, run tool_discovery with kind=\"mcp\" (or an MCP server name as source) to connect the servers, then pass the exact server name of an mcp status row. Configured secrets are redacted from the result.","arguments":{"server":{"description":"Exact MCP server name (from a `tool_discovery` mcp status row).","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"mcp_resource_templates","description":"List the resource templates of an MCP server. Before using it, run tool_discovery with kind=\"mcp\" (or an MCP server name as source) to connect the servers, then pass the exact server name of an mcp status row. Configured secrets are redacted from the result.","arguments":{"server":{"description":"Exact MCP server name (from a `tool_discovery` mcp status row).","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"mcp_resource_read","description":"Read an MCP resource by URI. Before using it, run tool_discovery with kind=\"mcp\" (or an MCP server name as source) to connect the servers, then pass the exact server name of an mcp status row. Copy an exact URI from mcp_resource_list or mcp_resource_templates. Configured secrets are redacted from the result.","arguments":{"server":{"description":"Exact MCP server name (from a `tool_discovery` mcp status row).","type":"string","is_required":true},"uri":{"description":"Exact resource URI, as returned by `mcp_resource_list` or\n`mcp_resource_templates`.","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"mcp_resource_subscribe","description":"Subscribe to update notifications for an MCP resource. Before using it, run tool_discovery with kind=\"mcp\" (or an MCP server name as source) to connect the servers, then pass the exact server name of an mcp status row. Copy an exact URI from mcp_resource_list. Configured secrets are redacted from the result.","arguments":{"server":{"description":"Exact MCP server name (from a `tool_discovery` mcp status row).","type":"string","is_required":true},"uri":{"description":"Exact resource URI, as returned by `mcp_resource_list` or\n`mcp_resource_templates`.","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"mcp_resource_unsubscribe","description":"Stop update notifications for an MCP resource. Before using it, run tool_discovery with kind=\"mcp\" (or an MCP server name as source) to connect the servers, then pass the exact server name of an mcp status row. Copy an exact URI from mcp_resource_list. Configured secrets are redacted from the result.","arguments":{"server":{"description":"Exact MCP server name (from a `tool_discovery` mcp status row).","type":"string","is_required":true},"uri":{"description":"Exact resource URI, as returned by `mcp_resource_list` or\n`mcp_resource_templates`.","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"mcp_prompt_list","description":"List the prompts of an MCP server. Before using it, run tool_discovery with kind=\"mcp\" (or an MCP server name as source) to connect the servers, then pass the exact server name of an mcp status row. Configured secrets are redacted from the result.","arguments":{"server":{"description":"Exact MCP server name (from a `tool_discovery` mcp status row).","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"mcp_prompt_get","description":"Get an MCP prompt and its messages. Before using it, run tool_discovery with kind=\"mcp\" (or an MCP server name as source) to connect the servers, then pass the exact server name of an mcp status row. Copy an exact prompt name from mcp_prompt_list. Configured secrets are redacted from the result.","arguments":{"arguments":{"description":"Prompt arguments, name to string value.","type":"object","is_required":false},"name":{"description":"Exact prompt name, as returned by `mcp_prompt_list`.","type":"string","is_required":true},"server":{"description":"Exact MCP server name (from a `tool_discovery` mcp status row).","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"terminal_session","description":"Drive an interactive program through a persistent terminal (PTY): TUIs, REPLs, pagers, editors, installers and prompts. Sessions persist across calls for this conversation; a running process is normal, and stop sessions when you are done.{{#if tool_names.shell}} `{{tool_names.shell}}` is for ordinary non-interactive commands.{{/if}}\n- `action`: `start` (needs `command`; `cols`/`rows` only here, default 120x40), `send` (`keys`), `look`, `stop`, `list`.\n- `keys` is comma-separated: named keys are `enter`, `tab`, `esc`, the arrows (`up`, `down`, `left`, `right`), `home`, `end`, `pgup`, `pgdn`, `backspace`, `delete`, `ctrl-c`, `ctrl-d`, `ctrl-l`, `ctrl-x`, `ctrl-z` and `f1` to `f12`; anything else is typed verbatim, and `comma` types a comma.\n- Each result has the rendered screen lines, cursor position and process state (`still_running`, `exit_status`). `lines_changed` and `screen_responded` compare against the previous observation: after `send`, change during the wait window after the keys (not proof the keys caused it); after `look`, passive change since last time.\n{{#if tool_names.terminal_snapshot}}\n- `{{tool_names.terminal_snapshot}}` renders the screen as a PNG when text cannot show spacing, colour, borders or clipping, and also resets that comparison baseline.\n{{/if}}\n- Under a jail (`-C DIR`) the command runs in the same jail as the shell tool.","arguments":{"action":{"description":"start, send, look, stop or list.","type":"string","is_required":true},"cols":{"description":"Columns (start only; default 120).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"command":{"description":"Command to run (start).","type":"string","is_required":false},"cwd":{"description":"Working directory (start; default the current directory).","type":"string","is_required":false},"keys":{"description":"Comma-separated keys to send (send).","type":"string","is_required":false},"rows":{"description":"Rows (start only; default 40).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"session":{"description":"Session name (default \"main\").","type":"string","is_required":false},"wait_ms":{"description":"Milliseconds to wait for output before reading the screen (default\n700, at most 10000).","type":"integer","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"terminal_snapshot","description":"Render the current screen of a terminal session as a PNG, for judging layout, borders, colours, cursor placement, wrapping or clipping that text cannot show. The session must already be started. The result is the image when the model accepts images, and always names the file it was written to.","arguments":{"session":{"description":"Session name (default \"main\").","type":"string","is_required":false},"wait_ms":{"description":"Milliseconds to wait for output first (default 100).","type":"integer","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"lsp","description":"Ask a language server about a source file: `diagnostics` (errors and warnings in the file), `definition`, `references` or `hover` (type and docs) at a position. The server is picked by extension from what is on PATH: rust-analyzer for Rust, basedpyright or pyright for Python, gopls for Go, typescript-language-server for TypeScript and JavaScript, clangd for C and C++. A file no installed server covers returns an error naming what was looked for.\n- Each call sends the file as it is on disk now, so call `diagnostics` after an edit to check it. The first call for a project starts the server and can take a while; later calls reuse it. A server still indexing may return nothing; call again.\n- A position is `line` and `character`, both 1-based.\n- Locations come back as `path:line:column: source line`, relative to the working directory when under it.","arguments":{"character":{"description":"1-based column (definition, references, hover).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"file_path":{"description":"File to query.","type":"string","is_required":true},"line":{"description":"1-based line (definition, references, hover).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"operation":{"description":"diagnostics, definition, references or hover.","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"kernel","description":"Execute Python in a persistent IPython kernel (Jupyter). State survives between calls: functions, imports, open connections and loaded data defined in one call are there in the next.\n- `action`: `run` (default) submits `code`; `poll` returns what a running cell produced since the last read; `wait` blocks for it up to `timeout`; `interrupt` stops the cell (the namespace survives); `restart` replaces the kernel (destroys every definition); `kill` shuts it down.\n- `timeout` (default 120 seconds) is the longest one call blocks. A cell that finishes inside it comes back whole; one still running comes back as a handle, `HANDLE <n> RUNNING`, and a new `code` is refused while it runs.\n- stdout, stderr, results and tracebacks come back; images are written to files and reported as `IMAGE <path>`. A finished cell ends with a `NAMESPACE` line listing the functions and classes defined so far.\n- Cells can call tools: the namespace holds `tools`, and `tools.<name>(...)` runs the tool `<name>` with keyword arguments and returns its result text. Each call goes through the same checks as a direct call (allowed tools, argument validation, bans, read-before-edit); a refusal raises `tools.ToolError`. `tools.names()` lists the callable tools; `kernel` and `toolbox` are not callable from a cell.\n{{#if tool_names.toolbox}}\n- `{{tool_names.toolbox}}` saves functions defined here to disk and loads them into later sessions.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if tool_names.shell}}\n- Use `{{tool_names.shell}}` for builds, tests, git and anything that is a command rather than Python.\n{{/if}}","arguments":{"action":{"description":"run (default), poll, wait, interrupt, restart or kill.","type":"string","is_required":false},"code":{"description":"Python code to run (run).","type":"string","is_required":false},"handle":{"description":"Handle of a run that is still going (poll, wait).","type":"string","is_required":false},"timeout":{"description":"Seconds a run (or wait) blocks before returning a handle (default 120).","type":"integer","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"toolbox","description":"Functions that outlive the session: save a function to disk, load saved functions into the kernel, keep every revision with its provenance.\n- `action`: `list` (default; every saved function with its latest revision and scope), `show` (source of the latest or a given `revision`), `save` (`name` plus `source`: one top-level `def name(...)`; `note` says what changed), `load` (execs every saved function, or just `name`, into the kernel), `history` (every revision of `name` with date, session and note), `delete` (removes `name`; its revisions stay in the history file).\n- `scope`: `global` (default, every project) or `project` (`.forge/toolbox/` in the working directory; shadows a global function of the same name).\n- `save` never overwrites: each save is a new revision (`r1`, `r2`, …). It refuses a function that reads a free name the source does not define or import (a session variable, an unsaved helper); the error names every one. Imports used by the function go inside its body or above it in `source`.\n{{#if tool_names.kernel}}\n- Until `load` has run, the `{{tool_names.kernel}}` namespace has none of the saved functions.\n{{/if}}","arguments":{"action":{"description":"list (default), show, save, load, history or delete.","type":"string","is_required":false},"name":{"description":"Function name (show, save, load, history, delete).","type":"string","is_required":false},"note":{"description":"What the revision changed (save).","type":"string","is_required":false},"revision":{"description":"Revision to show or load (default the latest).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"scope":{"description":"global (default) or project.","type":"string","is_required":false},"source":{"description":"Function source (save): one top-level `def`.","type":"string","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"browse","description":"Read a web page the way a browser sees it, JavaScript included, through obscura (a self-contained browser engine). Pages that build themselves in JavaScript come back rendered; rendered dumps use a five-second settle window.\n- `dump` (default `markdown`): `markdown` (links made absolute against the page URL), `text`, `html` (the DOM after scripts ran), `links` (URL, tab, anchor text per line), `original` (the raw response body as text), `assets` (one JSON object per sub-resource), `cookies` (the cookie jar as JSON).\n- `screenshot`: a path to save a PNG of the settled page; the result is then only the `SCREENSHOT path=… size=…` line, so call again without it to read the page.\n- A status of 400 or above leads the result with `ERROR: HTTP <code> from <url>` followed by the page.\n- Private, loopback and link-local IP literals are allowed. No POST, headers or body.\n{{#if tool_names.fetch}}\n- Use `{{tool_names.fetch}}` for API calls that need a method, headers or a body.\n{{/if}}\n- obscura comes from forge's managed binaries (`forge tools --install`) or, failing that, PATH.","arguments":{"dump":{"description":"markdown (default), text, html, links, original, assets or cookies.","type":"string","is_required":false},"screenshot":{"description":"Write a PNG screenshot to this path instead of returning the page.","type":"string","is_required":false},"url":{"description":"http(s):// or file:// URL.","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"browser_probe","description":"Render and screenshot a page or local HTML file in real headless Chromium (Playwright; WebGL through SwiftShader, no GPU needed) and measure whether an interaction changes it.\n- `click` is a `>`-separated chain of case-insensitive regexes matched against visible button or link text (e.g. `menu>settings`); `press` is a comma-separated list of keys pressed after the clicks.\n- The result lists the PNG frames (after load, after the actions, and `hold_ms` later) with per-frame metrics: size, dominant-colour share, unique colours, changed pixels against the previous frame, WebGL availability, console and page errors. Metrics describe the frames; they do not judge them.\n- Needs `python3` with the `playwright` package and its Chromium; a missing one is reported as an error.\n{{#if tool_names.read}}\n- `{{tool_names.read}}` a returned PNG to look at the frame.\n{{/if}}","arguments":{"click":{"description":"CSS selector to click after load.","type":"string","is_required":false},"hold_ms":{"description":"Milliseconds between the two frames after the action (default 1600).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"output_dir":{"description":"Directory for the frames (default a fresh directory under\ntool-results).","type":"string","is_required":false},"press":{"description":"Comma-separated keys to press after load (Playwright key names).","type":"string","is_required":false},"settle_ms":{"description":"Milliseconds to wait after load (default 1200).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"target":{"description":"URL or local HTML file to open.","type":"string","is_required":true},"viewport_height":{"description":"Viewport height (default 800).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"viewport_width":{"description":"Viewport width (default 1280).","type":"integer","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"serper_search","description":"Google search via Serper (needs `SERPER_API_KEY`). Keyword queries: exact phrases, names, error messages, recent news. Returns Google's organic results as title, URL and snippet, with the answer box first when there is one.\n{{#if tool_names.tavily_search}}\n`{{tool_names.tavily_search}}` returns extracted page text and a synthesized answer instead of snippets.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if tool_names.exa_search}}\n`{{tool_names.exa_search}}` finds things you can describe but not name.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if tool_names.docs_search}}\n`{{tool_names.docs_search}}` is for library documentation.\n{{/if}}","arguments":{"num":{"description":"Results (default 8).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"query":{"description":"","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"tavily_search","description":"Web search via Tavily (needs `TAVILY_API_KEY`). Each result carries extracted page text, usually with a one-paragraph synthesized answer first, so research and \"how do I\" questions get read without fetching each page.\n{{#if tool_names.serper_search}}\n`{{tool_names.serper_search}}` is for exact phrases and the freshest news.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if tool_names.exa_search}}\n`{{tool_names.exa_search}}` finds things you can describe but not name.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if tool_names.docs_search}}\n`{{tool_names.docs_search}}` is for library documentation.\n{{/if}}","arguments":{"max_results":{"description":"Results (default 8).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"query":{"description":"","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"exa_search","description":"Semantic web search via Exa (needs `EXA_API_KEY`): describe what you want in a full sentence and it finds pages by meaning, each with a slice of page text. Best when you know what you mean but not what it is called.\n{{#if tool_names.serper_search}}\n`{{tool_names.serper_search}}` is for exact strings, names and error messages.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if tool_names.tavily_search}}\n`{{tool_names.tavily_search}}` is for research questions that want a synthesized answer.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if tool_names.docs_search}}\n`{{tool_names.docs_search}}` is for library documentation.\n{{/if}}","arguments":{"num":{"description":"Results (default 8).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"query":{"description":"Describe what you want in a full sentence.","type":"string","is_required":true},"text_chars":{"description":"Characters of page text per result (default 1500).","type":"integer","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"docs_search","description":"Version-current library documentation via Context7 (`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` optional). Use it before writing code against a third-party library, framework, SDK or API. It resolves `library` to Context7's best match and returns documentation snippets with source URLs. Context7 matches keywords, so a hit is reported only when the requested name (case and separators ignored) is the hit's project name (an id path segment or its title) or one whole word of it; anything else comes back as no match rather than an unrelated project's docs. Set `topic`; an untargeted dump is long.\n{{#if tool_names.serper_search}}\n`{{tool_names.serper_search}}` is for issues, changelogs and discussion around a library.\n{{/if}}","arguments":{"library":{"description":"Library name, e.g. \"tokio\" or \"react\".","type":"string","is_required":true},"tokens":{"description":"Tokens of documentation to return (default 4000).","type":"integer","is_required":false},"topic":{"description":"What to look up in the docs.","type":"string","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"notebook_edit","description":"Change one cell of a Jupyter notebook (.ipynb): `replace` its source (the default), `insert` a new cell after it, or `delete` it. Read the notebook first{{#if tool_names.read}}; `{{tool_names.read}}` shows each cell's id as `[cell N id=ID type]`{{/if}}. A notebook changed on disk since the read is refused; read it again.\n- `cell_id` is the cell's id, or `index:N` (0-based) for notebooks without ids. `insert` with no `cell_id` inserts at the top and needs `cell_type` (`code` or `markdown`).\n- `new_source` is the cell's whole new source. A replaced code cell loses its outputs and execution count.\n- The notebook keeps its JSON layout (one-space indent, trailing newline).\n{{#if tool_names.undo}}\n- The notebook is snapshotted first, so `{{tool_names.undo}}` restores it.\n{{/if}}","arguments":{"cell_id":{"description":"The cell to edit or insert after: its id, or `index:N` (0-based).\nOmit with `insert` to insert at the top.","type":"string","is_required":false},"cell_type":{"description":"code or markdown (insert; replace keeps the type when omitted).","type":"string","is_required":false},"edit_mode":{"description":"replace (default), insert or delete.","type":"string","is_required":false},"file_path":{"description":"The `.ipynb` file.","type":"string","is_required":true},"new_source":{"description":"New cell source (replace, insert).","type":"string","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"wiki_convert","description":"Convert one file for a wiki vault: text, code and structured text are returned as text; PDFs through `pdftotext`; office and ebook formats through `pandoc` (or `soffice`); images come back as an embed plus any text `tesseract` reads from them (a missing tesseract is reported as OCR unavailable). Images and audio/video are copied into `<vault>/assets/` when `vault` is given (it must exist). The model decides what pages to write; this only converts input and copies media.","arguments":{"file_path":{"description":"File to convert.","type":"string","is_required":true},"vault":{"description":"Vault directory media is copied into (`<vault>/assets/`).","type":"string","is_required":false}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"wiki_write","description":"Create or replace a structured wiki page with front matter.\n- `kind` selects the page family: `source`, `entity`, `concept` or `synthesis`; the page goes to `<vault>/<kind>s/<slug>.md`.\n- `vault` must name an existing vault directory; a blank or `.` vault is refused.\n- An existing page is protected unless `overwrite=true`; pass a fully merged body when replacing.\n- Tags, type and update date are normalized by the tool. Use wikilinks in `body` where knowledge should connect.","arguments":{"body":{"description":"Page body (markdown, wikilinks welcome).","type":"string","is_required":true},"kind":{"description":"source, entity, concept or synthesis.","type":"string","is_required":true},"overwrite":{"description":"Replace an existing page.","type":"boolean","is_required":false},"tags":{"description":"Tags.","type":"array","is_required":false},"title":{"description":"Page title.","type":"string","is_required":true},"vault":{"description":"Existing vault directory.","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
<tool>{"name":"wiki_finish_ingest","description":"Archive an ingested unit, append the log entry and commit the vault when possible.\n- `source` is the ingested file under `<vault>/raw/`; it moves to `<vault>/raw/archive/`. Archive runs first; if it fails, no log entry or commit is created.\n- The log entry (date, source, `note`) is appended to `<vault>/log.md`.\n- The commit runs only when the vault is the root of its own git work tree and there is a diff; a vault nested inside another repository is not committed. A failed commit is an error, with the archive and log left in place to retry.","arguments":{"note":{"description":"What was added, or why the unit was skipped.","type":"string","is_required":true},"source":{"description":"The ingested file under `<vault>/raw/` to archive.","type":"string","is_required":true},"vault":{"description":"Existing vault directory.","type":"string","is_required":true}}}</tool>
